.TH DQL-QUERY 1 "2026-07-05" "dql 0.1.0" "DelightQL Manual"
.SH NAME
dql-query \- compile and execute a DelightQL query
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dql query
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fIQUERY\fR]
.br
.B dql q
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fIQUERY\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Compiles a DelightQL query to SQL and, by default, executes it and
prints the results. The query source is chosen in this order:
.IP \(bu 3
the
.I QUERY
argument, if present;
.IP \(bu 3
the file named by
.BR \-\-file ;
.IP \(bu 3
standard input, when stdin is not a terminal;
.IP \(bu 3
otherwise an interactive REPL is started.
.PP
The stdin path reads
.B until EOF
and blocks until the pipe closes; a notice announcing the wait (and
the escape hatches) is printed to stderr before the read, so a
harness whose pipe never delivers EOF has its diagnosis in the
captured stderr. Stdout stays reserved for results.
.PP
A file (or stdin) may contain multiple statements (with
.BR \-\-sequential ).
They execute in order; the
.B final
statement's result is what is returned \(em earlier statements execute
for their effects (mounts, consults, writes), not their output.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input
.TP
.BI \-\-file " FILE"
Read the query from
.IR FILE ;
a
.I FILE
of
.B \-
means stdin.
.TP
.B \-\-sequential
Execute multiple queries sequentially (for files with multiple
queries).
.TP
.BI \-\-attach " ATTACH"
Attach an external database.
.SS Compilation
.TP
.BI \-\-to " STAGE"
Inspect instead of executing. Every value but
.B results
(the default, the executing road) is an INSPECTION. Stages, in pipeline
order:
.BR cst ,
.BR ast\-unresolved ,
.BR ast\-resolved ,
.BR ast\-refined ,
.BR ast\-sql ,
.BR sql ,
.B results
(default),
.BR fingerprint ,
.BR hash ,
.BR totalhash .
.RB ( recursion\-depth
is deprecated; use sys::execution.stack(*) instead.)
.IP
A compile stage renders the statement's representation and executes
nothing. A digest stage runs the statement only if the compiler classifies
it effect-free and prints a digest of its result; a statement that would
execute an effect \(em a DML or DDL directive, a run, a session
directive such as
.BR mount! ,
an inline DDL block \(em is refused before anything runs
.RI ( semantic/effect/observation ),
and under
.B \-\-sequential
every statement of the sequence is judged. To execute a statement and
digest its result, keep the executing road and choose the digest as the
format:
.BR "\-\-to results \-f hash" .
.IP
.B hash
prints a column-name-independent data hash;
.B totalhash
includes column names;
.B fingerprint
prints result-fingerprint JSON for semantic comparison, naming the digest
version in its
.I digest
field. The same three digests are available as
.B \-f
formats on the executing road.
.IP
Every digest reads the cells the result delivered, never their display:
SQL NULL, an empty value, and the text NULL are three different cells,
and a BLOB contributes its exact bytes. Row order does not matter; a
repeated row counts each time. A cell carries no type, so an INTEGER 5
and a TEXT '5' digest identically.
.IP
A name the compiler invented \(em for an unnamed computation such as
.BR "age + 1" ,
or for columns whose names collide \(em is drawn fresh on every run: the
authored name the column lost, if it lost one, then the mark \(pp, then,
for a column nobody named, a word saying where its value came from, then
sixteen hexadecimal digits, as in
.I id\(pp80c43d5c2a8b6cd9
for a collision and
.I \(ppexpr_1b845432a344564f
for a computed value
.RI ( anon_
marks an unnamed anonymous-table cell). Printed headings and generated
SQL show the drawn spelling, so they differ between two runs of the same
query whenever the result holds one. This is deliberate: nothing may
depend on an invented name. The digests do not move:
.B totalhash
and
.B fingerprint
read each invented column as
.IR <mint:N> ,
numbering the invented columns of the heading left to right, and
.B hash
ignores names.
.IP
A query that fails to compile under
.B \-\-to
emits the same structured error record as normal execution \(em URI
and full message \(em and exits 1; the inspection surface never knows
less than the execution surface about why a compile failed. (Under
the hood both are projections of
.IR sys::execution.compile ,
whose relation carries
.I error
= the URI and
.I error_message
= the prose.)
.IP
Note that stage output is itself delivered as a one-column relation
named
.IR representation ,
so
.B \-\-to sql
in the default format prints a header line and the SQL with newlines
escaped as \en. Use
.B \-f list
for readable multi-line SQL, or
.B \-n
to drop the header.
.TP
.BI \-\-dialect " DIALECT"
Target dialect for the generated SQL. See
.BR dql (1).
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-format " FORMAT"
Output format. One of
.BR table ,
.BR box ,
.BR json ,
.BR jsonl ,
.BR csv ,
.BR tsv ,
.BR list ,
.BR raw ,
and the digests
.BR hash ,
.BR totalhash ,
.BR fingerprint .
The default is tab-separated values with a header row. A digest format
prints one machine value for the executed result \(em the statement runs,
effects included \(em and ships no mid-run
.B stdout!
set to the console; it is the executing counterpart of the
.B \-\-to
digest inspections, which refuse an effect.
.B json
emits one array;
.B jsonl
emits one object per row (streams; composes with jq). Both are typed:
a column DECLARED numeric whose text round-trips emits an unquoted
number, SQL NULL emits
.BR null ,
and a TEXT '007' stays "007" \(em a value that does not round-trip is
demoted to a string, never emitted as a corrupted number. An undeclared
column (an expression, a CTE result) takes the storage class of its
.I "first non-NULL"
value within the first 64 rows: a column whose first non-NULL value is
numeric emits numbers, and a column that shows no non-NULL value in that
window declares nothing and stays strings. A leading NULL no longer
decides the column's type, so
.B "5 ; null"
and
.B "null ; 5"
type identically. A genuinely mixed column remains order-dependent
(election picks one class; off-class values demote per-value) \(em
declare the column (or cast) when a consumer depends on it. Keys keep the
relation's column order.
.B csv
and
.B tsv
are also machine formats: each has one representation of absence a reader
can tell from any text. Like every format but the JSON pair, their text is
sanitized for a terminal unless
.B \-\-no\-sanitize
is given \(em a dangerous control byte is spelled
.B \exHH
before the format\(aqs own escaping, and a cell that itself spells
.B \ex
has its backslashes doubled so the spelling stays unambiguous; the default
output is a display of the value, the opt\-out its faithful serialization,
and both are injective. Tab, newline, carriage return, quotes and absence
are left to the encodings below, which distinguish the same values either
way. In
.B csv
SQL NULL is the empty unquoted field, the empty text is
.BR \(dq\(dq ,
and a field holding a comma, a quote, or a line break is quoted with its
quotes doubled. In
.B tsv
SQL NULL is
.BR \eN ,
and text escapes its own alphabet \(em a backslash as
.BR \e\e ,
a tab as
.BR \et ,
a newline as
.BR \en ,
a carriage return as
.BR \er
\(em so the text
.B NULL
stays
.B NULL
and every other byte is literal.
.B table
and
.B box
draw an aligned pipe-separated grid.
.B list
prints one
.I column = value
line per value with a blank line between rows.
.B raw
is byte-faithful extraction of exactly ONE column: cell bytes are
written verbatim with no separators (a separator would corrupt binary
data), NULL writes zero bytes, and rows concatenate as a byte stream.
Multi-column results are refused. Writing raw bytes to a terminal
prints a warning on stderr (control sequences pass through verbatim);
raw is intended for pipes and files \(em it is the one output path
that never converts bytes to text. With
.BR \-\-to " sql"
it instead prints the bare compile artifact with a trailing newline.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-no\-headers
Suppress the header row.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress the REPL banner and meta output. Only meaningful in
interactive mode; no effect on query output paths.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sanitize
Disable output sanitization (allows raw terminal control sequences
through).
.TP
.BI \-\-highlights " FILE"
Path to a highlights.scm file.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " FILE"
Path to a theme file.
.SS Database
.TP
.BI \-\-db " DATABASE"
SQLite database file. Optional; default is an in-memory database.
.TP
.B \-\-make\-new\-db\-if\-missing
Create the
.B \-\-db
file if it does not exist. Without this flag a missing file is a
refusal, not a creation. Global, like
.B \-\-db
itself \(em it also works with bare
.B dql
to start the REPL on a fresh database (see
.BR dql (1)).
.SS Instrumentation and gates
.TP
.BI \-\-debug " OPTS"
Debug options, comma-separated.
.TP
.BI \-\-danger " HIERARCHY=STATE"
Open a danger gate for this session. STATE is ON, OFF, ALLOW, or 1\-9.
Example:
.BR "\-\-danger cardinality/cartesian=ON" .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " HIERARCHY=STATE"
Set a config for this session. Same STATE grammar as
.BR \-\-danger .
.TP
.BI \-\-error\-prefix " PREFIX"
See
.BR dql (1).
.SS Replay
.TP
.BI \-\-replay\-repl " SCRIPT|TARBALL"
Re-run a recorded interactive session through the real prompt.
.I SCRIPT
is a
.B replay\-script.<MS>
file the REPL writes at exit (one block per input, headed
.BR "# <id> <kind>" );
.I TARBALL
is a
.B bug\-<MS>.tgz
from the REPL's
.B .bug
command, which also carries the session's
.B context.<MS>
(the original arguments and environment, honored, with database and DDL
paths redirected to the extracted copies).
The driver spawns
.B dql
on a pseudo\-terminal, types each dot command and pastes each block,
and waits for the prompt between keystrokes. The child's terminal output
is shown verbatim, the child writes its own session files, and the exit
status is the child's. Conflicts with
.I QUERY
and
.BR \-\-file .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Query compiled and executed.
.TP
.B 1
Compile or runtime error; one structured record per error on stderr:
.IP
.EX
<RS>[delightql-error://semantic/resolution/table] Table not found: nope
.EE
.IP
Feed the bracketed identifier to
.BR dql-explain (1)
for the full story.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ dql query --db app.db 'users(*), age > 30 |> (id, first_name)'
$ echo 'users(*) ~> count:(*)' | dql query --db app.db
$ dql query --db app.db --to sql -f list 'users(*), age > 30'
$ dql query --db app.db --to hash 'orders(*)'   # inspect: a pure query's data hash
$ dql query --db app.db -f hash 'rows(*) |> insert!(t(*))(*)'  # execute, then digest
$ dql query -f json '_(name, age @ "Ada", 36)'   # no db needed
$ dql query --db app.db --to sql -f raw 'users(*)'  # bare pasteable SQL
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dql (1),
.BR dql-explain (1),
.BR dql-format (1)
